Z||000000||isthiswhereiputausername?||12-17-2001||01:51 PM||||MY TCC quit working (wont go into overdrive) and it is still hooked up..  Is there any reason I should unhook the plug until I have a chance to replace it?<P>Its been this way a few weeks now, but have heard I should unhook it?<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||My7Fieros||12-17-2001||06:46 PM||||I had (have) an 87 GT which I recently found mine was unhooked..been driving it like that for a year or so no probs....<BR>BTW, are you sure its the TCC? A way to tell is while you're driving, use your left foot and slightly press the brake WHILE your right foot is still on the accelerator. If the tranny 'kicks down' and the rpms go up, then your TCC is fine.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||12-17-2001||07:47 PM||||First thing is to simply see why it's not working.... For a start...<P>unplug the tranny connector<BR>turn on the key<BR>probe the harness connector for 12V<P>If you have no power then the problem is a fuse or the brake pedal switch.<P>If you do have power, the problem is in the transmission or ECM. Most likely in the tranny. Either the solinoid is bad or the third gear switch is.<P>If you know which terminal on your ECM drives the TCC, then connect a meter between it and 12V. The meter should show 12V once the car is cruising over 35MPH. Don't use a test light. If it draws too much current you could damage the ECM.<P>You can double check that the ECM is calling for lock with an ECM scanner. The scanner usually can't tell you if the actual driver is working on Fiero ECMs. That's why you need the meter.<P>you can get cheap meters in any home improvement or K/Wall mart.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||isthiswhereiputausername?||12-18-2001||12:57 PM||||Thanks for the info  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>It dont seem to be working with the brake, So I will test voltage.<P>||||reg||1
